// RUN: not %gopp check %S/Inputs/inst-iface.gopp > %t.check 2>&1
// RUN: not %gopp build -emit=gir %S/Inputs/inst-iface.gopp > /dev/null 2> %t.build
// RUN: cmp %t.check %t.build
// RUN: FileCheck --implicit-check-not=error[E %s < %t.check
// RUN: not %gopp check %S/Inputs/inst-iface-tests.gopp > %t.tcheck 2>&1
// RUN: not %gopp build -emit=gir %S/Inputs/inst-iface-tests.gopp > /dev/null 2> %t.tbuild
// RUN: cmp %t.tcheck %t.tbuild
// RUN: FileCheck --check-prefix=TESTS --implicit-check-not=error[E %s < %t.tcheck
// P04.08 (decision M3): `gopp check` and the build report the same
// per-instance errors, also for a method that only a type descriptor or an
// itab needs (no call names it). The instance walk takes the whole method
// set of an instance of a generic type where a value of it becomes an
// interface value (the checker's record Info::IfaceConversions: a result, a
// variable, an argument, a struct, slice or map literal element, a
// conversion, a comparison with an interface, a send, an Option of an
// interface, `panic`) and where a type assertion or a type switch case names
// it, which is where the lowering makes the descriptor. The output of both is
// the same, byte for byte. The other side: in each file an instance that is
// only named (`var named W[Option[bool]]`) brings no method, so no error (n
// methods of n named instances are not n squared:
// GIRStress.NamedInstancesOfATypeBringNoMethods).
